_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"Every grid here is made of solid rectangles of colour 3 on a 0 background, with some colour‑9 dots scattered inside each rectangle. In every pair the output is the input with exactly one rectangle erased to 0, and the rest left untouched. The fact sheet confirms this: each pair's changed cells form one full rectangle, for example [1,1,3,5] in pair 1 and [7,6,11,12] in pair 3.\n\n**Which rectangle is erased?** Count the 9s inside each rectangle:\n- **Pair 1:** the erased block (rows 1–3, cols 1–5) has 2 nines. The other blocks have 3, 4, 4, 3 and 5.\n- **Pair 2:** the erased 5×5 block has 1 nine. The others have 4, 2, 2 and 5.\n- **Pair 3:** the erased 5×7 block has 3 nines. The others have 6, 4 and 5.\n- **Pair 4:** the erased 2×3 block has 2 nines, the fewest of any block.\n\nSize does not decide it: pair 2 erases a mid‑sized block and pair 4 erases the smallest one. The common rule is: **erase the rectangle that contains the fewest 9s.**\n\n**Test input.** Its blocks have these nine counts:\n- rows 1–3 / cols 2–7: 3\n- rows 0–5 / cols 9–13: 2\n- rows 5–10 / cols 0–4: 5\n- rows 8–11 / cols 6–10: 4\n- rows 10–14 / cols 12–14: 3\n- rows 12–14 / cols 0–3: 4\n\nThe minimum, 2, is unique, so the block at rows 0–5, cols 9–13 gets cleared.\n\nThe fact sheet calls the test background 3, but it is clearly 0 like every train pair (3 is just the more common colour there). So I keep 0 as the background constant, since it is the same in all pairs.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Find the rectangles: each is a 4‑connected component of non‑background cells, whatever its colour.\n2. Count the 9s inside each one.\n3. Fill the rectangle with the fewest 9s with background.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = 0\n    comps = components(g != bg)\n    best = None\n    for c in comps:\n        n9 = sum(1 for (y, x) in c if g[y, x] == 9)\n        if best is None or n9 < best[0]:\n            best = (n9, c)\n    out = g.copy()\n    if best is not None:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(best[1])\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = bg\n        emit(out, f\"Erase the block at rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1}: it holds the fewest 9s ({best[0]}).\")\n    else:\n        emit(out, \"No blocks found; grid unchanged.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Find the rectangles: each is a 4‑connected component of non‑background cells, whatever its colour."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 4:** the erased 2×3 block has 2 nines, the fewest of any block."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Size does not decide it: pair 2 erases a mid‑sized block and pair 4 erases the smallest one."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The common rule is: **erase the rectangle that contains the fewest 9s.**"}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Which rectangle is erased?** Count the 9s inside each rectangle:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Test input.** Its blocks have these nine counts:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Count the 9s inside each one."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"960a7e8f9625fbbf1dc73e33e8d6774b44000794fb0a90fdbf294aadd471ed5d","task_sha256":"ed9b91424f93ef8aa93b6593a5da0582200517e1b1f3b95e06e7588856842e98","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
